Three things worth flagging in the §4–§7 content:

- **`quickbench.py:322-353`** — `_cell_record`'s docstring says a failed cell carries "no number at all", but the code copies `decode_tps` from the row with no status test, and `report.summarize` retains values on non-PASS rows by design. The app prints that number beside the status unguarded (`ResultsView.swift:246`). No test asserts the FAIL case. This is §7 Q1.
- **`measure.py:1063-1070`** — `_warmup_rate` sums `completion_tokens or 0` after checking only `came_back`, so a returned request with a null token count silently contributes 0 tokens to the batch aggregate instead of refusing the step (§7 Q7).
- **`quickbench.py:508`** — `clock=time.time` is used for `actual_s` durations while every measurement path uses `time.monotonic` (§7 Q9).

The three map lines I am least sure of:

1. `transport.py:110-114` / `:117-145` — §1 says `_connection` is "HTTPConnection over `_parts`" and `_call_before_deadline` "runs a blocking call on a daemon thread, waits on a deadline". I confirmed both by reading, but I did not verify the `_parts` call inside `_connection` produces the *path* tuple element `_parts` returns rather than a modified one.
2. `measure.py:1201-1207` — §1 says "one TokenCounter per cell id, cached; construction failure recorded and the cell goes N/A". I read `_token_counter`'s caching and error capture, but not the call site that converts the recorded error into N/A, so the "goes N/A" half is inferred.
3. `report.py:1555-1588` — §1 says `_check_workload_pins` compares "same shape set, same `messages` and `max_tokens` per shape". I read the docstring and signature at `:1555-1562` and the `ignore_messages` argument, but not the loop body, so the claim that every other field of every shape is compared is from the docstring, not the code.

